        "At the small Bethel Bible College in Topeka, Kansas, the holiness preacher Charles Fox Parham sets his students to search the Book of Acts for the evidence of the baptism in the Holy Spirit. On 1 January 1901 the student Agnes Ozman speaks in tongues as Parham lays hands on her, and others soon follow. Parham was the first to teach that speaking in tongues is the initial evidence of Spirit baptism — the doctrine at the heart of classical Pentecostalism, which reached Los Angeles through his student William J. Seymour.",
