        "The Scottish-Australian faith healer John Alexander Dowie, who had built a large healing ministry in Chicago, opens Zion City on the shore of Lake Michigan in 1901 as a theocratic Christian community without alcohol, tobacco, doctors or pork. Dowie's empire collapsed by 1906, but when Charles Parham brought the Pentecostal message to Zion that year, many of its residents embraced it. Zion supplied a remarkable number of early Pentecostal leaders, among them John G. Lake and F. F. Bosworth, and its 'Zionist' churches spread to southern Africa.",
